 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Requires the power authority of the state of New York to file a report annually commencing March 31, 2022 with the governor, the comptroller, the chair of the assembly ways and means and the senate finance committees, respectively, and the authority budget office, on its operations and transactions, complying with the uniform system of accounts required for electric corporations pursuant to the public service law.",
 "title": "Prescribes uniform system of accounts for power authority of the state of New York",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A435",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A435-2019",
     "printNo": "A435",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 196\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 435, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  public  authorities  law,  in  relation to\n      prescribing a uniform system of accounts for the  power  authority\n      of the state of New York\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  amend the Public Authorities Law (\"PAL\") to require\nthe Power Authority of the State of New York (\"Power Authority\"), begin-\nning March 31, 2022, to file an annual report with specified state offi-\ncials and entities that \"shall conform, in like manner\"  to  the  annual\nreport  filed  by  private electric corporations with the Public Service\nCommission (\"PSC\") pursuant to Public Service Law (\"PSL\") Section 66(6),\nand comply with the \"uniform system of accounts\" (\"USOA\") applicable  to\njurisdictional electric corporations.\n \n  The  supporting  memorandum suggests that the Power Authority's annual\nreport is comparable to the annual report that private corporations send\nto their shareholders, and the report this  bill  would  require  \"could\nshed  much  more light on the Power Authority's operations than does the\nannual report which they [sic] are now required to submit.\"\n \n  This bill has been advanced in one form or another for decades and was\nlong ago rendered moot by statute.\n \n  The PAL was amended in 2005 and  again  in  2009  in  connection  with\ncomprehensive  reform  measures  intended  to  increase transparency and\naccountability of public authorities. As a result, the PAL now  requires\nall  public  authorities, including the Power Authority, to make compre-\nhensive and detailed annual reports, financial  disclosures,  and  other\nannual  filings  in  accordance with a format prescribed by Article 9 of\nthe PAL and regulations of the New York State Comptroller.  Accordingly,\nthe report this bill would require would serve no useful purpose.\n \n  I am also advised that the new reporting and  accounting  requirements\nwould  be  extremely  disruptive  of  the Power Authority's business and\ncause the Power Authority to incur significant start-up  costs  and  new\nrecurring  annual  costs  which,  for the reasons described above, would\nprovide no corresponding benefit. Finally, this bill suffers from  tech-\nnical deficiencies.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 196,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2019
   }
  ],
  "size": 1
 },
